 ### Histogram Packing
-A compression technique applied to images with sparse histograms of intensity levels to improve compression ratios, specifically in volumetric medical images [S2]. It addresses sparse histogram issues that cause bitrate deterioration in entropy estimation heuristics [S2]. It is compliant with JP3D via the Non-Linear Transformation (NLT) defined in JPEG 2000 Annex K, allowing the original histogram to be restored in the decoder pipeline [S2]. Performance: HP alone (HP+DWT) yields an average bitrate improvement of 5.34% across the test set. For sparse histogram images (MRI1, MRI3), it improves compression ratio by over 20%. HP alone improves the compression ratio of sparse histogram images by almost 29% on average [S2]. Pitfall: Sparse histograms make standard entropy estimation ineffective without HP [S2]. Parameter: The decoder must support the Non-Linear Transformation (NLT) extension defined in JPEG 2000 Part 2 (Annex K) to restore the original histogram [S2].
+A compression technique applied to images with sparse histograms of intensity levels to improve compression ratios, specifically in volumetric medical images [S2]. It addresses sparse histogram issues that cause bitrate deterioration in entropy estimation heuristics [S2]. It is compliant with JP3D via the Non-Linear Transformation (NLT) defined in JPEG 2000 Annex K, allowing the original histogram to be restored in the decoder pipeline [S2]. Performance: HP alone (HP+DWT) yields an average bitrate improvement of 5.34% across the test set. For sparse histogram images (MRI1, MRI3), it improves compression ratio by over 20% [S2]. HP alone improves the compression ratio of sparse histogram images by almost 29% on average [S2]. Pitfall: Sparse histograms make standard entropy estimation ineffective without HP [S2]. Parameter: The decoder must support the Non-Linear Transformation (NLT) extension defined in JPEG 2000 Part 2 (Annex K) to restore the original histogram [S2].
 
 ### Node Usage Tracking via Bit Packing
-A GPU-side tracking mechanism that packs node IDs into a compact `uvec4` array for CPU-GPU synchronization, using bit-shifting to store 3D coordinates and a boolean flag [S3, unofficial]. The algorithm increments `nodeUsage` by `1u` to avoid `0,0,0` ambiguity with the root node, then packs `x,y,z` into a single `uint` via bit shifts: `x<<20 | y<<10 | z` [S3, unofficial]. Bits are stored in `NodeUsageList`, incrementing `curComponentIndex`. If `curComponentIndex` reaches `k_NumComponentsPerList` (4), it resets to 0 and increments `curListIndex`. If `curListIndex` reaches `k_NumNodeLists` (3), it wraps to 0 [S3, unofficial]. Parameters: `k_MaxNodesPerPixel = 12` (3 lists × 4 components); `k_NumNodeLists = 3`; `k_NumComponentsPerList = 4` [S3, unofficial]. Layout: `NodeUsageList` is a flat array of 3 `uvec4`s (12 components total). Each `uvec4` stores packed node data. Each component holds 30 bits: bits 0-9 for x, 10-19 for y, 20-29 for z. The w component is unused in packing but stored in `nodeUsage` [S3, unofficial]. Pitfall: The w component of `nodeUsage` is explicitly excluded from packing due to corruption issues when OR-ing with bit 30, requiring CPU-side subtraction of `1u` to recover original IDs [S3, unofficial].
+A GPU-side tracking mechanism that packs node IDs into a compact `uvec4` array for CPU-GPU synchronization, using bit-shifting to store 3D coordinates and a boolean flag [S3, unofficial]. The algorithm increments `nodeUsage` by `1u` to avoid `0,0,0` ambiguity with the root node, then packs `x,y,z` into a single `uint` via bit shifts: `x<<20 | y<<10 | z` [S3, unofficial]. Bits are stored in `NodeUsageList`, incrementing `curComponentIndex` [S3, unofficial]. If `curComponentIndex` reaches `k_NumComponentsPerList` (4), it resets to 0 and increments `curListIndex` [S3, unofficial]. If `curListIndex` reaches `k_NumNodeLists` (3), it wraps to 0 [S3, unofficial]. Parameters: `k_MaxNodesPerPixel = 12` (3 lists × 4 components); `k_NumNodeLists = 3`; `k_NumComponentsPerList = 4` [S3, unofficial]. Layout: `NodeUsageList` is a flat array of 3 `uvec4`s (12 components total). Each `uvec4` stores packed node data. Each component holds 30 bits: bits 0-9 for x, 10-19 for y, 20-29 for z [S3, unofficial]. The w component is unused in packing but stored in `nodeUsage` [S3, unofficial]. Pitfall: The w component of `nodeUsage` is explicitly excluded from packing due to corruption issues when OR-ing with bit 30, requiring CPU-side subtraction of `1u` to recover original IDs [S3, unofficial].
 
